  <p>
    <strong>Infantile spasms</strong> are not an adrenal disease and not an immune disease.
    They are an epileptic encephalopathy of the first year or two of life: clusters of
    brief flexor or extensor seizures, and on EEG a high-voltage, disorganised pattern
    called hypsarrhythmia. The usual epilepsy drugs often do nothing. Most children who
    have had the syndrome have later developmental disability, and a shorter delay before
    effective treatment is associated with a better developmental outcome.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The condition is on this site because the drug with the longest track record against
    it is a melanocortin. Repository corticotropin — purified ACTH in a slow-release gel —
    is labelled in the United States as monotherapy for infantile spasms in infants and
    children under two.<Cite n={1} /> That is a sharper indication than the gel’s labels for{' '}
    <a href="/sarcoidosis">sarcoidosis</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/multiple-sclerosis-relapse">acute multiple-sclerosis relapses</a>, and the
    evidence behind it is stronger.
  </p>

  <h2>A hormone, not an immune modulator</h2>
  <p>
    ACTH is cut from <a href="/pomc">POMC</a>. It binds <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> on
    the adrenal cortex, and the cortex releases cortisol. For infantile spasms that is the
    mechanism to take seriously: the peptide is being used to raise corticosteroids in a
    developing brain, which is why oral steroids are the comparison treatment and why the
    arguments about steroid-independent melanocortin receptors on immune cells — live on
    the <a href="/therapeutics#corticotropin">therapeutics page</a> for the inflammatory
    uses — do not carry over cleanly here.<Cite n={3} /> Nobody has shown that the
    anti-spasm effect survives in the absence of a cortisol rise.
  </p>

  <h2>What the trials can say</h2>
  <p>
    The 2012 American Academy of Neurology and Child Neurology Society review is the
    clearest public summary, and it has to be dated. The Academy has since retired the
    guideline, so the recommendations are no longer the Academy’s current advice. The
    evidence it assembled is still the evidence.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    On that review, ACTH was more effective than vigabatrin for short-term control of
    spasms, excluding children whose spasms come from tuberous sclerosis, where vigabatrin
    may be the better drug. Hormonal therapy — ACTH or prednisolone — was also the option
    associated with better later development when the spasms were cryptogenic, meaning no
    cause had been found. A Class I randomised trial inside that review compared high-dose
    and low-dose natural ACTH and did not find a clear difference in stopping the spasms,
    clearing hypsarrhythmia, or relapse. The review was explicit that there was not enough
    evidence to call other steroids equivalent to ACTH.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Read that as a ranking of old options, not as a recipe. Doses, preparations (natural
    gel versus synthetic fragments), and what “response” means differ across the studies.
    The label is not a protocol, and this page will not turn it into one.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this label is different from the others</h2>
  <p>
    Acthar’s inflammatory indications were written in an era before controlled trials were
    the bar, and the modern studies that exist for sarcoidosis and MS relapse are small.
    Infantile spasms is the indication where randomised evidence, a paediatric age limit,
    and the word “monotherapy” all show up together.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={2} /> It is also
    the place where the cost argument around the gel has been sharpest, because the
    alternative is a cheap steroid and the patient is an infant.<Cite n={3} /> Cost is not
    efficacy. It is part of why the indication is argued about.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Infantile spasms are the melanocortin system’s oldest job in neurology, and the job is
    still the adrenal one: ACTH in, cortisol out. For the hormone, see <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>;
    for the receptor, <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>; for where this sits against every
    other labelled and experimental use, the <a href="/conditions">conditions map</a>.
  </p>

